---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import PaintingCredit from "../../components/PaintingCredit.astro";
import { getPainting, paintings } from "../../lib/paintings";
import { siteConfig } from "../../site-config";

const hero = getPainting("landscapes");
const named = paintings.filter((painting) => painting.attribution === "named");
const attributed = paintings.filter((painting) => painting.attribution === "attributed");
const after = paintings.filter((painting) => painting.attribution === "after");
---

<ContentLayout
  title="The plates"
  description="The six paintings this site is built from, and what each museum is willing to claim about who made them."
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroImageAlt={hero.alt}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <p>
    Every page on this site opens on a painting. They are not decoration, and
    they are not a set of Tang Yins. Sorted by what the holding museum is
    actually willing to say, {named.length} of them are given to Tang Yin
    outright, {attributed.length} is qualified as attributed to him, and
    {" "}{after.length} has no artist at all.
  </p>

  <p>
    The label under each image is transcribed from the museum's own catalogue
    record, not written here. That is the point of showing them together. An
    attribution is a claim made by an institution at a particular moment, with
    a particular amount of evidence behind it, and reading it as a fact about
    the object is the habit this course spends twelve weeks trying to break.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two things worth noticing before you scroll. The Metropolitan Museum of Art
    gives Tang Yin's dates as 1470 to 1524 and the Cleveland Museum of Art gives
    them as 1470 to 1523, so the field does not fully agree on when he died. And
    every image here is published by its museum as public domain or CC0, which
    is why the course can reproduce them at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Given to Tang Yin</h2>

  {
    named.map((painting) => (
      <figure class="plate">
        <a href={painting.url} rel="noopener">
          <Image
            src={painting.image}
            alt={painting.alt}
            widths={[400, 800]}
            sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 40rem, 90vw"
            class="plate-image"
          />
        </a>
        <PaintingCredit painting={painting} />
      </figure>
    ))
  }

  <h2>Attributed to Tang Yin</h2>

  <p>
    Cleveland's published label for this scroll opens "Attributed to Tang Yin",
    where its label for Mount Hua above just says "Tang Yin". Two words, one
    catalogue, and a different claim. The museum is telling you it thinks this
    is his and that it is not prepared to say so flatly. Notice how easily that
    qualifier disappears when a painting gets reproduced somewhere with a
    shorter caption.
  </p>

  {
    attributed.map((painting) => (
      <figure class="plate">
        <a href={painting.url} rel="noopener">
          <Image
            src={painting.image}
            alt={painting.alt}
            widths={[400, 800]}
            sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 40rem, 90vw"
            class="plate-image"
          />
        </a>
        <PaintingCredit painting={painting} />
      </figure>
    ))
  }

  <h2>After Tang Yin</h2>

  <p>
    The Met records this scroll with two artist entries. One is "Unidentified
    artist". The other is "After Tang Yin", which is a statement about what the
    painting follows rather than about who held the brush. Nothing here says it
    is a forgery. It says the museum has not found anyone to name, and that the
    resemblance is close enough to file under his name anyway. Both of those are
    findings, and they are different findings.
  </p>

  {
    after.map((painting) => (
      <figure class="plate">
        <a href={painting.url} rel="noopener">
          <Image
            src={painting.image}
            alt={painting.alt}
            widths={[400, 800]}
            sizes="(min-width: 60rem) 40rem, 90vw"
            class="plate-image"
          />
        </a>
        <PaintingCredit painting={painting} />
      </figure>
    ))
  }
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .plate {
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-xl);
    margin-inline: 0;
  }

  .plate-image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 32rem;
    object-fit: contain;
    object-position: center;
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
  }

  .plate a:focus-visible .plate-image {
    outline: 2px solid var(--at-link);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
</style>
